  "live_records": {
    "q-340506": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340506",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Let $G(x)=x+1$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $g(x)$ is the $4$-periodic extension of $G(x)$ from $x\\in [0,4)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $g(x+8)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $g(x)=G(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $g(x+2)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $g$ is the $4$-periodic extension of $G,$ we have \n\n- $g(x+4)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $g(x)=G(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,4).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x+8)=g((x+4)+4)=g(x+4)=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=5.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(5)=g(1+4)=g(1)=G(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G(5)=5+1=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x)\\ne G(x)$ for $x=5.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=1.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1+2)=g(3)=G(3)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1)=G(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x+2)\\ne g(x)$ for $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
